msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:22
msgid "Creating a map with contour lines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:24
msgid "Plotting a contour map is handled by :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdcontour`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:28
msgid ""
"import pygmt\n"
"\n"
"# Load sample earth relief data\n"
"grid = pygmt.datasets.load_earth_relief(resolution=\"05m\", region=[-92.5, "
"-82.5, -3, 7])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:40
msgid "Create contour plot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:42
msgid ""
"The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdcontour` method takes the grid input. It plots "
"annotated contour lines, which are thicker and have the elevation/depth "
"written on them, and unannotated contour lines. In the example below, the "
"default contour line intervals are 500 meters, with an annotated contour "
"line every 1,000 meters. By default, it plots the map with the equidistant "
"cylindrical projection and with no frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:50
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdcontour(grid=grid)\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:62
msgid "Contour line settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:64
msgid ""
"Use the ``annotation`` and ``levels`` parameters to adjust contour line "
"intervals. In the example below, there are contour intervals every 250 "
"meters and annotated contour lines every 1,000 meters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:70
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdcontour(grid=grid, annotation=1000, levels=250)\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:82
msgid "Contour limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:84
msgid ""
"The ``limit`` parameter sets the minimum and maximum values for the contour "
"lines. The parameter takes the low and high values, and is either a list (as "
"below) or a string ``limit=\"-4000/-2000\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:90
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdcontour(grid=grid, annotation=1000, levels=250, limit=[-4000, "
"-2000])\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:102
msgid "Map settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:104
msgid ""
"The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdcontour` method accepts additional parameters, "
"including setting the projection and frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:109
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdcontour(\n"
"    grid=grid,\n"
"    annotation=1000,\n"
"    levels=250,\n"
"    limit=[-4000, -2000],\n"
"    projection=\"M10c\",\n"
"    frame=True,\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:128
msgid "Adding a colormap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:130
msgid ""
"The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdimage` method can be used to add a colormap to "
"the contour map. It must be called prior to :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdcontour` "
"to keep the contour lines visible on the final map. If the ``projection`` "
"parameter is specified in the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdimage` method, it does "
"not need to be repeated in the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdcontour` method. "
"Finally, a colorbar is added using the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.colorbar` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/contour_map.rst:139
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.grdimage(grid=grid, cmap=\"gmt/haxby\", projection=\"M10c\", "
"frame=True)\n"
"fig.grdcontour(grid=grid, annotation=1000, levels=250, limit=[-4000, "
"-2000])\n"
"fig.colorbar(label=\"Elevation\", unit=\"m\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

